## CHAPTER 6 — THE MULTIPLANETARY COORDINATION PROBLEM

### 6.1 The Ultimate Coordination Test

A multiplanetary civilization introduces a constraint that no prior coordination technology has addressed: **physical latency imposed by the speed of light**. Unlike institutional friction, cognitive bandwidth, or trust deficits — all of which are in principle addressable by sufficiently sophisticated technology or culture — signal propagation delay is a physical constant.

Multiplanetary systems face three persistent constraints: long and variable latency, intermittent connectivity, and limited opportunities for repair or centralized supervision. Under these conditions, intelligence is most valuable when it supports resilient coordination at scale: routing and prioritizing information across heterogeneous nodes, forecasting and managing environmental risks, and maintaining long-term cultural and operational continuity. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2512.22399)

### 6.2 The Autonomous Aligned Coordination Problem

**Definition 6.1 (Interplanetary Coordination System).** An **Interplanetary Coordination System** is a tuple $(\mathcal{A}_E, \mathcal{A}_M, \mathcal{V}, \tau_{\min}, \tau_{\max})$ where:
- $\mathcal{A}_E$ is the Earth population
- $\mathcal{A}_M$ is the Mars population
- $\mathcal{V}$ is a shared value structure encoding the collective objectives of both populations
- $[\tau_{\min}, \tau_{\max}] = [3\text{ min}, 22\text{ min}]$ is the one-way signal delay range

**Definition 6.2 (Autonomous Aligned Coordination).** The **Autonomous Aligned Coordination (AAC) Problem** is the problem of designing a decentralized decision protocol $\Pi = (\Pi_E, \Pi_M)$ such that:

1. **(Autonomy):** $\Pi_M$ enables $\mathcal{A}_M$ to make all time-critical decisions locally, without waiting for Earth confirmation.

2. **(Alignment):** The joint action sequence produced by $(\Pi_E, \Pi_M)$ converges to the joint optimum of $\mathcal{V}$ with probability $\geq 1 - \epsilon$ for some small $\epsilon > 0$.

3. **(Robustness):** $\Pi_M$ maintains alignment under full communication blackout periods of duration up to $T_{\max}$ (e.g., solar conjunction: $\approx$ 2 weeks).

**Theorem 6.1 (AAC Impossibility in the Centralized Limit).** No centralized coordination protocol — one in which all decisions require Earth approval — can solve the AAC Problem for time-critical decisions with response requirement $r < 2\tau_{\min} = 6$ minutes.

*Proof.* By physical necessity: any Earth confirmation takes at minimum $2\tau_{\min}$. Decisions requiring response in $r < 2\tau_{\min}$ cannot receive Earth confirmation before the decision deadline. $\blacksquare$

**Theorem 6.2 (Value Pre-Loading Condition).** The AAC Problem is solvable if and only if $\mathcal{V}$ can be represented as a finite, learnable structure $\hat{\mathcal{V}}$ such that a rational agent with access to $\hat{\mathcal{V}}$ and local state $s_M$ can compute the Earth-aligned optimal action $x_M^*(s_M) = \arg\max_{x_M} \mathbb{E}_{x_E}[V(x_M, x_E) | s_M, \hat{\mathcal{V}}]$ without further Earth communication.

*Proof.* Sufficiency: if such $\hat{\mathcal{V}}$ exists, $\Pi_M$ is defined by evaluating $x_M^*(s_M)$ locally. Necessity: if no such representation exists, then for some state $s_M$ the optimal action cannot be determined without Earth-specific information not available locally, violating autonomy. $\blacksquare$

This theorem is the formal statement of a profound civilizational insight: **the problem of multiplanetary governance is identical to the problem of value alignment in AI systems**. Both require encoding a rich, high-dimensional value structure into a finite, transferable representation that can guide autonomous agents in the absence of real-time supervision. The interplanetary coordination problem and the AI alignment problem are isomorphic.

### 6.3 Decentralized Trust Protocols for Interplanetary Coordination

A multiplanetary civilization requires communications infrastructure that tolerates delay and disruption. Blockchain-based decentralized trust mechanisms provide a foundation for autonomous yet aligned coordination across interplanetary distances by enabling decentralized trust without requiring centralized control. [Originstamp](https://originstamp.com/blog/reader/blockchain-multiplanetary-civilization/en)

However, standard blockchain consensus protocols (e.g., Nakamoto consensus, PBFT) require sub-second to sub-minute confirmation times and are therefore physically incompatible with Earth-Mars communication delays. We require a new class of **Delay-Tolerant Consensus** (DTC) protocols.

**Definition 6.3 (Delay-Tolerant Consensus).** A DTC protocol $\Pi_{\text{DTC}}$ achieves consensus among geographically separated nodes with heterogeneous delays if, for any finite maximum delay $\tau_{\max}$, the protocol reaches agreement with probability $1 - \delta$ within time $O(\tau_{\max} \log N)$.

**Theorem 6.3 (DTC Existence).** Delay-Tolerant Consensus is achievable under asynchronous Byzantine fault tolerance provided $f < N/3$ nodes are faulty, where the confirmation latency is $O(\tau_{\max} \cdot \lceil \log_{2/3} \delta \rceil)$.

*Proof.* By extension of the Castro-Liskov PBFT protocol to asynchronous networks with message delay $\tau_{\max}$: standard PBFT requires $3f+1$ nodes for safety and liveness with $f$ Byzantine faults. Replacing synchrony assumptions with $\tau_{\max}$-bounded delays produces the stated complexity. $\blacksquare$

### 6.4 Governance Structures for Multiplanetary Societies

Drawing on the institutional history of the International Telecommunication Union and Universal Postal Union, effective Mars governance institutions require five design principles: institutional neutrality, flexibility and renegotiation mechanisms, Earth-based precedent-setting before departure, multi-stakeholder boards with weighted voting, and governance structures that scale appropriately without imposing Earth-level bureaucratic overhead. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2602.08853)

We formalize these as properties of a governance structure $\mathcal{G} = (\mathcal{R}, \mathcal{D}, \mathcal{E}, \mathcal{P})$ where $\mathcal{R}$ is the rule set, $\mathcal{D}$ is the decision procedure, $\mathcal{E}$ is the enforcement mechanism, and $\mathcal{P}$ is the power allocation.

**Axiom IP1 (Latency-Aware Decision Classification).** $\mathcal{D}$ must classify decisions into tiers by response requirement $r$:

$$T_k = \{d \in \mathcal{D} \mid 2\tau_{k-1} \leq r(d) < 2\tau_k\}$$

where $\tau_0 = 0$, $\tau_1 = \tau_{\min} = 3\text{ min}$, $\tau_2 = \tau_{\max} = 22\text{ min}$, $\tau_3 = \infty$. Tier $T_1$ decisions require fully local Mars authority. Tier $T_2$ decisions permit Earth consultation. Tier $T_3$ decisions require joint Earth-Mars deliberation.

**Axiom IP2 (Value Compression).** $\mathcal{G}$ must include a protocol for compiling $\mathcal{V}$ into a portable, auditable representation $\hat{\mathcal{V}}$ that can be pre-loaded into Mars governance agents before communication blackout.

**Axiom IP3 (Divergence Monitoring).** $\mathcal{G}$ must include a continuous monitoring protocol that tracks $d_{\text{KL}}(\mathcal{V}_E \| \mathcal{V}_M)$ — the divergence between Earth and Mars value structures — and triggers a reconciliation protocol when this divergence exceeds a threshold $\theta_{\mathcal{V}}$.

