APPENDIX A — MATHEMATICAL FOUNDATIONS OF COORDINATION THEORY: COMPLETE PROOFS

A.1 Preliminaries and Notation

We recall the primitives from Chapter 2. Let $\mathcal{A} = \{a_1,\dots,a_N\}$ be a finite set of agents. For each agent $a_i$ we have a state space $\mathcal{S}_i$, a strategy space $\mathcal{X}_i$, and a utility function $u_i : \prod_j \mathcal{X}_j \to \mathbb{R}$. A coordination graph $G=(\mathcal{A},E)$ represents feasible direct communication links.

A.1.1 Formalization of Coordination Event. The definition of a Coordination Event in Chapter 2 requires that there exists a joint action profile $x^*$ whose sum of utilities strictly exceeds the sum obtainable in any Nash equilibrium. While the original statement (Definition 2.1) refers to a single Nash equilibrium $x^{\text{NE}}$, we need to make this rigorous: the Nash equilibrium profile might not be unique, and the comparison should be against the best Nash equilibrium in terms of total welfare. We refine the definition as follows.

Definition A.1 (Coordination Event, Precise). Given a game $(\mathcal{A}, (\mathcal{X}_i), (u_i))$, let $\mathcal{E} \subseteq \prod_i \mathcal{X}_i$ denote the set of (pure-strategy) Nash equilibria. A Coordination Event occurs if there exists a profile $x^*$ such that
\sum_i u_i(x^*) > \sup_{x^{\text{NE}} \in \mathcal{E}} \sum_i u_i(x^{\text{NE}}).


If $\mathcal{E} = \emptyset$ (no Nash equilibrium exists), the condition is vacuously satisfied for any profile yielding finite utility; the supremum over an empty set is $-\infty$ by convention. This captures the idea that coordination generates surplus beyond the best non-cooperative outcome.

A.2 Proof of Theorem 2.1 (Coordination–Channel Duality)

Theorem 2.1 (Restated). The maximum rate at which a population $\mathcal{A}$ can achieve aligned collective action is upper-bounded by the minimum cut capacity of the coordination network:
\mathcal{C}(\mathcal{A}) \le \min_{S \subset \mathcal{A}} \mathrm{Cut}(S, \mathcal{A}\setminus S) = \sum_{\substack{i\in S, j\notin S \\ (i,j)\in E}} C_{ij}.

Proof. We model the coordination process as information transmission over the directed (or undirected) graph $G=(\mathcal{A},E)$ with edge capacities $C_{ij}$ (the Shannon capacity of the channel between $i$ and $j$). Any joint coordinated action requires that all agents ultimately possess a common, sufficiently accurate understanding of the relevant state of the world and of each other’s intended actions. In information-theoretic terms, a minimum amount of mutual information must be established among all agents.

Consider any cut $(S, \bar S)$ partitioning the agent set. For agents in $S$ to coordinate with agents in $\bar S$, every bit of coordination-relevant information that originates in $S$ must traverse the cut at least once. By the max-flow min-cut theorem for network information flow (see, e.g., Ahlswede et al., “Network Information Flow”, IEEE Trans. Inf. Theory, 2000), the total rate at which information can be reliably transmitted from $S$ to $\bar S$ cannot exceed the sum of capacities of edges crossing the cut. This holds even if coding across multiple links is allowed, provided we consider independent channel uses. For a noisy coordination channel, the mutual information across the cut is bounded by the cut capacity.

Now let $R$ be the total coordination rate (in bits per unit time) that the system attempts to sustain. The coordination protocol will require that for any partition, the information flow across the cut is at least $R$, because all agents need to resolve their action choices jointly. Thus a necessary condition for $R$ to be achievable is $R \le \sum_{i\in S, j\notin S} C_{ij}$ for every cut $S$. Taking the minimum over all cuts yields $R \le \min_S \mathrm{Cut}(S,\bar S)$. Therefore, no coordination protocol can sustain a rate exceeding this min-cut value. $\blacksquare$

Remark. This proof assumes that coordination is a form of multi-terminal information transmission and that the underlying channels are memoryless and independent. In practice, coordination may involve interactive protocols with feedback; however, even with feedback, the min-cut bound remains a fundamental limit on the sum rate of common information generation, as shown in the theory of common randomness generation.

A.3 Formal Elaboration of Coordination Entropy (Proposition 2.1)

Proposition 2.1 (Restated). A civilization maximizes adaptability when coordination entropy $H_{\text{coord}}(\mathcal{A})$ is maximized subject to its constraint set. Low $H_{\text{coord}}$ corresponds to brittleness.

Proof sketch refined. Let $\mathcal{G}$ be the set of all joint action profiles (configurations) that the population can adopt given its communication, trust, and institutional constraints. Define $H_{\text{coord}} = - \sum_{g\in\mathcal{G}} p(g) \log p(g)$, where $p$ is the probability distribution over accessible configurations reflecting the system's potential to realise them under perturbations. By Jaynes' maximum entropy principle, subject to known constraints (e.g., mean resource consumption, social norms, etc.), the most unbiased distribution — the one that assigns the highest entropy — corresponds to the largest volume of achievable states. If $H_{\text{coord}}$ is low, the system is constrained to a narrow subset of configurations; a perturbation that pushes the required response outside this subset leads to failure. Thus adaptability (the ability to cope with novel environmental demands) is a monotone function of $H_{\text{coord}}$. $\blacksquare$

