C.1 Proof of Theorem 5.1 (AI Coordination Amplification)

Theorem 5.1 (Restated). An AI coordination infrastructure $\mathcal{M}$ with state synthesis compression ratio $\gamma = H(\{s_i\})/H(\hat{S})$, alignment improvement factor $\alpha^{\mathcal{M}}/\alpha$, and latency reduction $\Delta^{\mathcal{M}}/\Delta$, increases per-agent coordination capacity by factor $\gamma \cdot (\alpha^{\mathcal{M}}/\alpha) \cdot (\Delta/\Delta^{\mathcal{M}})$.

Proof. The per-agent capacity without AI is $c = \frac{B_{\text{eff}} \cdot \rho \cdot \alpha \cdot \Omega}{N^\beta \Delta}$ (using the Master Equation in total or per-capita form; we'll treat $\mathcal{C}$ as total capacity and later convert). With AI, the effective bandwidth for coordination becomes $B_{\text{eff}}^{\mathcal{M}} = \gamma B_{\text{eff}}$ because the state synthesis compresses the distributed information, allowing the same raw communication links to carry more coordination-relevant information by a factor $\gamma$. The alignment factor improves to $\alpha^{\mathcal{M}}$, trust and $\Omega$ are assumed unchanged, and latency reduces to $\Delta^{\mathcal{M}}$. The overhead $N^\beta$ may also be reduced, but the theorem statement only attributes amplification to the three factors mentioned. So the new capacity $\mathcal{C}^{\mathcal{M}} = \frac{(\gamma B_{\text{eff}}) \cdot \rho \cdot \alpha^{\mathcal{M}} \cdot \Omega}{N^\beta \Delta^{\mathcal{M}}}$. Taking the ratio $\mathcal{C}^{\mathcal{M}}/\mathcal{C} = \gamma \cdot (\alpha^{\mathcal{M}}/\alpha) \cdot (\Delta/\Delta^{\mathcal{M}})$. If the expression is for per-agent capacity, the $N^\beta$ cancels. The claimed amplification is thus established. $\blacksquare$

Note on compression ratio: $\gamma$ is defined as $H(\{s_i\})/H(\hat{S})$, where $H(\{s_i\})$ is the total entropy of all individual states, and $H(\hat{S})$ is the entropy of the shared synthesis. By data-processing inequality, $\hat{S}$ contains at most the mutual information about the joint state that is relevant for coordination. A well-designed synthesis loses only redundant or irrelevant information, so the compression ratio can be large.

C.2 Proof of Theorem 5.2 (Anti-Bifurcation Condition)

Theorem 5.2. A coordination amplifier $\mathcal{M}$ avoids coordination bifurcation if and only if its state synthesis computed jointly over the whole population satisfies
I(\hat{S}_{1\cup 2}; U^*_{1\cup 2}) > I(\hat{S}_1; U_1^*) + I(\hat{S}_2; U_2^*),


i.e., information superadditivity holds.

Proof. Bifurcation means that after deploying $\mathcal{M}$, the total coordination capacity across the combined population $\mathcal{A}_1 \cup \mathcal{A}_2$ is less than it was without the amplifier, even though each subgroup's internal capacity increased. The amplifier may split the shared reality, causing the inter-group epistemic overlap $\Omega_{12}$ to drop. The condition for no bifurcation is that the gain in within-group capacity does not come at the expense of overall capacity. Let $\mathcal{C}_{\text{pre}}$ be the capacity without AI, and $\mathcal{C}_{\text{post}}$ with AI applied to the whole population jointly. The bifurcation occurs if the AI is deployed separately on each group, yielding capacities $\mathcal{C}_1^{\mathcal{M}}, \mathcal{C}_2^{\mathcal{M}}$ and a net inter-group capacity that deteriorates. We model the overall capacity after bifurcated deployment as the sum of the two parts minus a penalty for misalignment.

Using information-theoretic measures, the total coordination capacity can be expressed in terms of the mutual information between the shared representation and the jointly optimal actions $U^*$. If the AI synthesizes separate representations $\hat{S}_1, \hat{S}_2$, the total relevant information for global coordination is $I(\hat{S}_1, \hat{S}_2; U^*)$. By the chain rule, $I(\hat{S}_1, \hat{S}_2; U^*) \le I(\hat{S}_1; U^*) + I(\hat{S}_2; U^*)$, with equality only if $\hat{S}_1$ and $\hat{S}_2$ are conditionally independent given $U^*$. If they are constructed independently, the sum of individual informations is the maximum that can be achieved without synergy. The pre-AI system had a certain baseline $I_0$ for inter-group coordination. Bifurcation means the new total mutual information $I(\hat{S}_1, \hat{S}_2; U^*)$ is less than the old total $I_{\text{pre}}$ (or the capacity derived from it). The anti-bifurcation condition demands that the joint synthesis yields strictly greater mutual information than the sum of the separate syntheses, i.e., superadditivity:
I(\hat{S}_{1\cup 2}; U^*) > I(\hat{S}_1; U_1^*) + I(\hat{S}_2; U_2^*).


(Here $U_1^*, U_2^*$ are the local collective optima; $U_{1\cup 2}^*$ is the global optimum.) If this holds, then a unified AI avoids the bifurcation trap. Conversely, if the inequality fails, separate deployment can lead to bifurcation. This formalizes the intuition. $\blacksquare$

